  "worked_solution": "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)\u2248f(a,b)+\u2207f(a,b)\\cdot \u27e8x-a,y-b\u27e9.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(3,2)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & \u2248f(3,2)+\u2207f(3,2)\\cdot \u27e8x-3,y-2\u27e9 \\\\  & =f(3,2)+\u27e8{f}_{x}(3,2),{f}_{y}(3,2)\u27e9\\cdot \u27e8x-3,y-2\u27e9 \\\\  & =9+\u27e8{f}_{x}(3,2),{f}_{y}(3,2)\u27e9\\cdot \u27e8x-3,y-2\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find ${f}_{x}(3,2)$ and ${f}_{y}(3,2)$ using the given partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(3,2) & =-\\frac{3(3)}{\\sqrt{26-(3)^{2}-2(2)^{2}}}=-3 \\\\ {f}_{y}(3,2) & =-\\frac{6(2)}{\\sqrt{26-(3)^{2}-2(2)^{2}}}=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y)\u22489+\u27e8-3,-4\u27e9\\cdot \u27e8x-3,y-2\u27e9.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can estimate $f(3.05,2.05)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=3.05$ and $y=2.05,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3.05,2.05) & \u22489+\u27e8-3,-4\u27e9\\cdot \u27e83.05-3,2.05-2\u27e9 \\\\  & =9+\u27e8-3,-4\u27e9\\cdot \u27e80.05,0.05\u27e9 \\\\  & =9-0.15-0.2 \\\\  & =8.65\\end{aligned}\n$$",
